\section{Discussion and limitations}\label{sec:discussion}
\paragraph{What an evaluator can take from this.}
A few runs give most of the information, and the choice of benchmarks matters at $k\le3$ and hardly beyond $k\approx10$. Reaching 90\% of the gap takes one run on HELM MMLU and nine or more on MTEB, with wide intervals and a headline $k^*$ biased low. Models that are later or stronger than the history are predicted worse, and new families are predicted worse on HELM MMLU but not on MTEB.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
(1) The results are for dense pools that we selected; the models outside the MTEB pool are larger, newer and stronger, and the missing-cell experiments do not go below 21\% observed cells, use the identity transform and test only models with at least 30 observed cells; missingness that concentrates on weaker models defeats selection in the history sweep (Appendix~\ref{app:history}). (2) The MTEB models are embedding models and the MMLU subjects are subtasks of one benchmark, so nothing here covers language-model benchmarks in general (Appendix~\ref{app:small}). (3) The conclusions on $k^*$ are fragile on both MTEB pools (wide intervals, censoring, dependence on predictor, level and transform), and the headline $k^*$, a minimum over two predictors, is biased low. (4) Closure is relative to LOBO, not to the noise floor, and families come from model names (organisations give the same conclusions, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:robust}). (5) The risk formula gives the shape of the error-versus-$k$ curve, not its level on real data (an offset of unknown cause); the coherence argument does not explain our data, why random selection is near-greedy at small $k$ is a hypothesis, and greedy selection has no proof. (6) Intervals ignore the choice of pool.
